Make bgfx's per-view GPU profiler work on GLES, and affordable on a tile GPU.

bgfx already has per-view timing: Profiler in src/renderer.h fills
Stats::viewStats and every backend already drives it. Two things stop it being
usable.

1. It reports nothing on any GLES device. glQueryCounter, glGetQueryObjectiv,
   glGetQueryObjecti64v and glGetQueryObjectui64v are not core in GLES -- they
   come from EXT_disjoint_timer_query with EXT suffixes, and that is the only
   spelling a GLES driver exports. The dynamic-import block asks for the
   unsuffixed names only, so the pointers stay null, m_timerQuerySupport is
   forced false, and both Stats::gpuTime* and the whole profiler go quiet.

   An EXT line after each core line is enough: GL_EXTENSION only assigns when
   the pointer is still NULL, so desktop GL keeps the core entry point and GLES
   falls through to the EXT one. This must go in the BGFX_USE_GL_DYNAMIC_LIB
   block -- the GLES-only block further down is #elif !BGFX_USE_GL_DYNAMIC_LIB,
   dead on every platform that loads GL through EGL/GLX/WGL, and there
   GL_EXTENSION resolves to a direct declaration rather than a dlsym.

2. It times every view, every frame. That is not viable on a tile-based GPU: a
   timestamp between two views sharing a framebuffer stops the driver merging
   them into one render pass, so each boundary becomes a tile flush and reload.
   Measured on Mali-G610, enabling BGFX_DEBUG_PROFILER turned a steady 26 ms
   frame into repeated ~29 s stalls of a single frame each, with audio resyncing
   after every one.

   Time one view per frame instead, advancing round-robin. Two timestamps per
   frame rather than two per view, and any sampling window covers every view
   many times over -- which is what a profiler averages across anyway. Note
   Profiler::end() no longer re-checks m_enabled: begin() only sets m_queryIdx
   when it started a query, so the guard was redundant and now would be wrong.

